However, some of our device plugins may need more information about this request. Here is some user stories.

1. I'm now developing a new network device plugin. When the plugin receives a `Allocate` request from kubelet, **it needs the pod name and namespace so it could retrieve the Pod from kube-apiserver or kubelet read-only port**. Our plugin may add some different configurations, like **different Envs or Mounts**, about this Pod based on the field `pod.spec.hostNetwork`.
2. I'm now developing a custom scheduler framework plugin so that scheduler could allocate devices for a Pod by adding device IDs in the pod annotations after scheduling. Our device pluin could know the fine-grained device scheduling result from pod annotations If device plugin could know the pod name and namespace.

In conclusion, I think pod base information, i.e. name and namespace could be passed to device plugins in order to elevate extensibility of device plugins while this patch does not need to make any modifications for device plugin gRPC interface. We only need to call a gRPC context method like `metadata.AppendToOutgoingContext(ctx, "pod", podNamespacedKey)` [here](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/40741681a24a5acf9361ec74b97e887a9da3e3e4/pkg/kubelet/cm/devicemanager/endpoint.go#L85-L123).

