In resource-topology-exporter, they are [calculating the amount of hugepage resources](https://github.com/k8stopologyawareschedwg/resource-topology-exporter/blob/d641fcbb7548070041a1afdda4b87230d8661f72/pkg/sysinfo/sysinfo.go#L63
) by calculating the total amount of bytes that the sum number of pages represent.

They are doing so because upstream Kubernetes is using bytes as the unit instead of pages, and I'm not sure why upstream is doing that... hugepages should be treated more like indivisible CPUs that are allocated to a specific consumer and cannot be shared amongst multiple consumers. Making the unit of hugepages equal to the amount of memory a) makes hugepages look just like the memory resource (which is shareable across consumers) and b) is confusing to applications that consume hugepage resources since those applications typically request X number of hugepages, not Y amount of bytes.

### What did you expect to happen?

Treat hugepage resources as pages, not bytes of memory.

