We encountered some very unexpected behavior while working with the [DelayingQueue](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/5a99930ab57b2c7837960b21d2c92e958db8c377/staging/src/k8s.io/client-go/util/workqueue/delaying_queue.go#L68).
Calling `q.AddAfter(item, 5*time.Hour)` and `q.AddAfter(item, 10*time.Millisecond)` will result in a single queue item being added after 10ms.
However, calling `q.AddAfter(item, 5*time.Hour)` and `q.AddAfter(item, 0*time.Millisecond)` results in two  items added, one after 0ms and one after 5h (instead of just once item at 0ms).

The same happens when using `q.Add(item)` instead of `q.AddAfter(item, 0*time.Millisecond)`.

This behavior can also be triggered when using the RateLimitingQueue in combination with an update event from eg. an informer.

The cause is a short-circuit that is triggered [here](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/5a99930ab57b2c7837960b21d2c92e958db8c377/staging/src/k8s.io/client-go/util/workqueue/delaying_queue.go#L241-L245) or [here](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/5a99930ab57b2c7837960b21d2c92e958db8c377/staging/src/k8s.io/client-go/util/workqueue/delaying_queue.go#L315-L317). Ideally, in both places, there would be a "remove from heap" operation. Similarly, `q.Add(...)` does not trigger a "remove from heap" operation.

The following test code can be used to test this behavior (add this to `delaying_queue_test.go`):
```go
func TestDeduping(t *testing.T) {
	fakeClock := testingclock.NewFakeClock(time.Now())
	q := NewDelayingQueueWithConfig(DelayingQueueConfig{Clock: fakeClock})

	first := "foo"
	initialDelay := 1 * time.Millisecond // 0ms makes the test fail here.

	q.AddAfter(first, initialDelay+0*time.Millisecond)
	if err := waitForWaitingQueueToFill(q); err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("unexpected err: %v", err)
	}
	q.AddAfter(first, initialDelay+50*time.Millisecond)
	if err := waitForWaitingQueueToFill(q); err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("unexpected err: %v", err)
	}

	// step past the first block, we should receive now
	fakeClock.Step(10 * time.Millisecond)
	if err := waitForWaitingQueueToFill(q); err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("unexpected err: %v", err)
	}
	if q.Len() != 1 {
		t.Error("should have added")
	}
	item, _ := q.Get()
	q.Done(item)

	// step past the second add
	fakeClock.Step(50 * time.Millisecond)
	if err := waitForWaitingQueueToFill(q); err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("unexpected err: %v", err)
	}
	if q.Len() != 0 {
		t.Errorf("should not have added")
	}
}
```

